  note = {Read the first 100,000 of about 280,000 characters. Checked: three mutually intelligible standards (Iranian Persian, Dari, Tajik); Old, Middle, New Persian as one language (Lazard); oldest Old Persian inscription Behistun; Middle Persian official, religious and literary language of the Sasanian empire (Lazard 1975), Aramaic-derived Pahlavi and Manichaean scripts; New Persian texts in Arabic script from the 9th century; emergence from the Ctesiphon court dialect and Khorasan (Paul 2000; Perry 2011); subperiods Early (8th/9th c.), Classical (10th to 18th c.), Contemporary (19th c. on); Mughal sole official language, gradual replacement from 1843; heavy medieval borrowing from Arabic; influence on Turkic, Armenian, Georgian, Greek, Indo-Aryan (Holes 2001); Persian loanwords in Hindi-Urdu, Punjabi, Kashmiri, Bengali, Sindhi; the page gives Dari official both "since 1964" and "since 1958" in different sections. Tertiary source; replace with Encyclopaedia Iranica "Persian language i. Early New Persian" (Paul) and related entries. Text CC BY-SA 4.0; nothing copied.}
}

